{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall not skew judgment; you shall not show partiality; and you shall not take a bribe.</b> Since it does not say 'and you shall not show partiality' with a vav, and it says 'and you shall not take a bribe' with a vav, infer from this that 'you shall not skew' and 'you shall not show partiality' are each an independent prohibition on its own, but 'you shall not take a bribe' is a derivative of 'you shall not show partiality,' because the wealthy are called 'the face of the earth,' as Rashi explained in parashat Miketz (Genesis 41:56), and when it says 'you shall not show partiality,' that means the wealthy who are called 'the face of the earth.' And when you do not show them partiality, then automatically 'you shall not take a bribe,' for one who shows partiality to the wealthy does so for the sake of the bribe he hopes to receive from them. And the language of 'skewing' (הַטָּיָה) teaches that the two litigants should be equal in his eyes, as though they were both placed on the pans of a balanced scale, without any pan tilting or inclining toward either side. And this is the meaning of 'you shall not show partiality' (לֹא תַכִּיר פָּנִים) — that it should be as though he does not recognize either one of them at all." "And as for the expression 'bribe' (שֹׁחַד), our Sages of blessed memory said (Ketubot 105a) that it is 'one' (חַד) — for the one who receives and the one who give become a single person, and he then no longer sees any liability on his own part. And this is not clear, for behold שֹׁחַד is the name of the money and not the name of the owners; and is it because it causes the owners to become as one that the money is called by their name? Rather, it appears to me that it is 'one' (חַד) in the sense of a sharpened point, from the expression ״בַּרְזֶל בְּבַרְזֶל יָחַד״ (Proverbs 27:17). For money is like a sharp knife that cuts swiftly. And every judge needs to be deliberate so that he does not cut the judgment swiftly. And when he receives the bribe, he knows at once toward which side the judgment inclines, and cuts it swiftly, like one who cuts with a sharp knife. And it is not the judge who cuts the judgment, but the money that cuts — and it is sharp (חַד). And because he alluded here that the judge must be deliberate in judgment, and through this one may come to the point of delaying justice by postponing it more than is proper, therefore it says ״צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף״. For the prophet indicts Israel by saying ״צֶדֶק יָלִין בָּהּ״ (Isaiah 1:21), in the manner we explained above in parashat Devarim (Deuteronomy 1:18) — that in capital cases they would conclude the case on that same day for acquittal but not for conviction, and they reversed the practice and would lodge the justice overnight — that is, the acquittal. Concerning this it says here: 'justice you shall pursue,' for the expression of 'pursuit' (רְדִיפָה) connotes swiftness, that he should hasten to cut the judgment once his merit — that is, his 'justice' (צֶדֶק) — becomes clear. And it doubled צֶדֶק: one for itself, and one to diminish the conviction that one is obligated to lodge overnight." "Another matter: 'bribe' (שֹׁחַד), which is sharp (חַד) like a sword that cuts — so too does the perversion of justice bring a sharp sword to the world, as is found in Avot (5:11): a sword comes to the world for the delaying of justice and for the perversion of justice. Therefore the bribe that perverts justice is called שֹׁחַד, because it is sharp (חַד) like a sword. And afterward he also warned against the delaying of justice, saying: ״צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף״ — that he should hasten to declare, 'So-and-so, you are acquitted,' to lay bare his justice, ״לְמַעַן תִּחְיֶה״ — and you will be saved from the sword; ״וְיָרַשְׁתָּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — Rashi explained: the appointment of qualified judges is worthy to give life to Israel and to settle them on their land. And the reason for 'to give life' (לְהַחֲיוֹת) is that were it not for the fear of the judges, 'man would swallow his fellow alive' (Avot 3:2); therefore they said that the appointment of judges causes life to persist — that is, that no man should swallow his fellow alive — and 'to settle them on their land,' as it is written (Proverbs 29:4): ״מֶלֶךְ בְּמִשְׁפָּט יַעֲמִיד אָרֶץ״. But the corruption of judges causes the destruction of the earth, and the generation of the Flood proves it. And so Jethro said: ״וְגַם כָּל הָעָם עַל מְקוֹמוֹ יָבֹא בְשָׁלוֹם״ (Exodus 18:23) — he should have said 'each man will sit in his place in peace'; rather, he spoke of the general place designated for all the people who at that time desired to come there, and Jethro said that through the merit of appointing judges they would merit to come there." "And by way of allusion one may say that he mentioned here both מִשְׁפָּט and צְדָקָה, because sometimes a judge does both justice and righteousness at once, in the manner that King David did — justice for this one and righteousness for that one (Sanhedrin 6b). And there is a distinction between מִשְׁפָּט and צְדָקָה: judgment (מִשְׁפָּט) needs to be done with deliberation, but righteousness (צְדָקָה) with pursuit and swiftness, as it is written (Proverbs 21:21): ״רוֹדֵף צְדָקָה וָחֶסֶד יִמְצָא חַיִּים״. And it is written (Isaiah 56:1): ״שִׁמְרוּ מִשְׁפָּט וַעֲשׂוּ צְדָקָה״. And 'guard' (שִׁמְרוּ) is the language of guarding and straining, for a beverage left standing on its dregs for a long time — the dregs fall to the bottom and the beverage remains clear, as it is said (Psalms 73:21): ״כִּי יִתְחַמֵּץ לְבָבִי״ etc.; so too one who is deliberate in judgment causes all the errors that cloud the intellect to be distanced. 'And do righteousness' (וַעֲשׂוּ צְדָקָה) — immediately, without delay; give to him at once and do not put him off. So too it is said here: ״לֹא תִקַּח שֹׁחַד״ — for it is sharp and cuts the judgment swiftly; rather, one must be deliberate in the matter of judgment (מִשְׁפָּט). But righteousness (צְדָקָה) is not so — rather: ״צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף״ — with pursuit and swiftness; ״לְמַעַן תִּחְיֶה״ — for 'one who pursues righteousness and kindness finds life'; ״וְיָרַשְׁתָּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ — for the judges will settle them on their land, and 'a king through justice establishes the earth.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {2 "The reference to Avot 3:2 follows the numbering in tractate Avot where this teaching appears in some editions as 3:2 and in others as 3:4; the Kli Yakar cites it as אבות ג ב."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:05.516312427Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}